==============================================================================
STEP-1 SUMMARY (session 13)
==============================================================================
METHOD VALIDATED: psi_n shift-invariance exact (0.000 deg) over integer + fractional
shifts incl. the session-8 26-sample lag. LS complex-harmonic fit is leakage-free.

INTERNAL CHECK PASSED: the model's ceiling-H3 and clipper-H3 are ~180 deg apart
(178.7/165.5/179.8 deg at 110/220/1000) by COHERENT complex subtraction — session
12's anti-phase interference re-derived from PHASE, independent of amplitude.

TRAP FOUND + AVOIDED: 'ceiling-only via high clipSat' is INVALID — the D1/D2 clamp
window tracks satLo (clampHi=9.6-satLo), so clipSat>~10 freezes node W into a DC
source and the residual output is just the harmonic-free clean BLEND bleed. Used
coherent subtraction of valid renders instead.

VERDICT: AMBIGUOUS / FREQUENCY-DEPENDENT.
  * 1000 Hz (H3=3000, capSNR 47 dB, notch-free, the ONLY strictly-conclusive tone):
    capture MATCHES the clipper (8 deg) and OPPOSES the ceiling (161 deg)
    -> ceiling odd term BACKWARDS at that frequency.
  * 220 & 440 Hz: capture leans the OTHER way (toward the ceiling / right-sign),
    but 220's H3 (660 Hz) sits on the bridged-T notch whose DEPTH the linear model
    gets wrong (model -28 vs capture -3.4 dB) -> the model H3 phase there is
    confounded by a KNOWN linear error, not necessarily nonlinear phase. 440 has
    poor clipper-ref SNR and unresolvable refs (68 deg).
  * 110 Hz: capture H3 SNR only 5 dB -> unusable.

So a single 'ceiling too big' vs 'ceiling backwards' answer does NOT hold across
frequency, and the one clean tone disagrees with the (contaminated) others. Two
readings are consistent with the data and cannot be separated by phase alone:
  (a) the nonlinear H3 phase is genuinely FREQUENCY-DEPENDENT (a static shaper of
      any family is structurally inadequate) — the step-2 static-vs-dynamic test
      decides this directly; OR
  (b) the frequency spread is linear-model phase error (the 717 Hz notch, SK
      corners) contaminating the per-tone capture-vs-model comparison.
Per the agreed protocol, step 1 alone does NOT pick a shape family. Step 2 is the
decider. Phase-aware (complex) targets are mandatory in any future fit regardless,
since interference nulls are otherwise a hidden confounder (the sessions-7-12 cycle).
